Hi Kyle,
I'm trying to classify my data set using the code described in normal vs. cancer example.

El viernes, 24 de julio de 2015, 20:13:05 (UTC+2), kbemis escribió:
> Hi Edu,
>
> Thank you for using Cardinal! For this, you will want to use the select() method in Cardinal. It works the same as the image() method, but triggers R's locator() function to allow you to select a region of interest, and then returns a logical vector indicating the selected pixels.
>
> For example, you would do:
>
> normal <- select(mydata, mz=XXX) # select pixels around the normal region of the image
> cancer <- select(mydata, mz=YYY) # select pixels around the cancer region of the image
> diagnosis <- as.character(rep(NA, ncol(mydata)))
> diagnosis[normal] <- "normal"
> diagnosis[cancer] <- "cancer"
> diagnosis <- as.factor(diagnosis)
>

My data set contain seven tumoral tissues, some of those responding to a certain therapy and some others not responding to the same therapy. I would like to assign "responder" and "non responder" for each tisssue but I don't know how to use the select method in this situation. My data set is combined using combine method and each tissue in this combined data set is called A,B, C, D, E, F, G. How wolud you apply:  diagnosis <- as.character(rep(NA, ncol(mydata))); diagnosis[normal] <- "normal" ; diagnosis[cancer] <- "cancer"; diagnosis <- as.factor(diagnosis) in my situation? Do I need to combine all the selections made with the select method?  Could you help me, please?

Thanks in advance

Paco

